  "summary": "A NULL pointer dereference in Node.js HTTP/2 lets an unauthenticated client crash the server, over both TLS and…",
  "details": "A NULL pointer dereference in Node.js HTTP/2 lets an unauthenticated client crash the server, over both TLS and cleartext, by cancelling file downloads. When the server serves a file with stream.respondWithFile() or stream.respondWithFD() and the client cancels the request, a StreamPipe used for the transfer is torn down in an order that leaves a NULL pointer which is then dereferenced, aborting the process. No crafted frames are needed, and a handful of cancelled requests against a default file-serving handler is enough. This vulnerability affects Node.js 26.x, 24.x, and 22.x.\n\n---\n- nodejs <unfixed>\nhttps://nodejs.org/en/blog/vulnerability/november-2026-security-releases#http2-cancelling-one-respondwithfile-download-crashes-the-server-null-dereference-in-streampipe-cve-2026-123875---medium",
